Yes, $M = \mathbb{T}^n$ necessarily. Here is the proof.

**Proof.**

Since $M$ is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{T}^n$, it is an $n$-dimensional (compact) manifold. As a submanifold of $\mathbb{T}^n$ (which is also $n$-dimensional), the inclusion map $\iota: M \hookrightarrow \mathbb{T}^n$ is an immersion with $\dim M = \dim \mathbb{T}^n = n$. Hence the differential $d\iota_p: T_pM \to T_p\mathbb{T}^n$ is an isomorphism at every $p \in M$, so by the inverse function theorem $\iota$ is a local diffeomorphism. In particular, $\iota(M) = M$ is **open** in $\mathbb{T}^n$.

On the other hand, $\mathbb{T}^n$ is compact, so $M \cong \mathbb{T}^n$ is compact. Since $\mathbb{T}^n$ is Hausdorff, every compact subset is closed; thus $M$ is **closed** in $\mathbb{T}^n$.

Now $M$ is both open and closed in $\mathbb{T}^n$. The torus $\mathbb{T}^n = (\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z})^n$ is connected, so the only nonempty subset that is both open and closed is $\mathbb{T}^n$ itself. Since $M$ is nonempty (it is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{T}^n$), we conclude

$$M = \mathbb{T}^n. \qquad \blacksquare$$

